1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
This is Doing and
Feeling, the long version.

2
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:05,560
Episode two: Minds in Other Bodies.

3
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:10,490
In two thousand two, researchers at Oxford
published a short paper in Science about

4
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:12,480
a New Caledonian crow named Betty.

5
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:17,070
Betty and a male companion, Abel, were
offered a little bucket of food at the bottom

6
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:21,480
of a vertical tube, along with two pieces
of wire, one straight and one hooked.

7
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:23,340
Abel flew off with the hook.

8
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:28,270
Betty picked up the straight wire, wedged
one end, bent it into a hook, and fished

9
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:30,880
out the bucket. She did
it again on later trials.

10
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:35,970
It looked like insight: a bird solving a
new problem by making a tool it had never

11
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:41,030
been taught to make. Fourteen years later,
a team led by Christian Rutz complicated

12
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:46,800
the story. Some wild-caught New Caledonian
crows, kept briefly in field aviaries,

13
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:51,330
bent straight twigs without needing
to, and one of them used Betty's exact

14
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:56,210
wedge-and-pull technique. Bending may
simply be part of the species' toolkit, so

15
00:00:56,260 --> 00:01:00,030
Betty may have been running a routine
rather than having a flash of insight.

16
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,680
The authors did not rule insight out.

17
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:06,660
Either way, the story carries
the lesson of this whole episode.

18
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:11,870
Here's the argument. Intelligence has
evolved again and again, in very different

19
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:17,350
bodies and brains: in birds, in octopuses,
in insects, and in simpler forms in cells

20
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:22,470
and collectives. It is a family of solutions
to the problems of staying alive, not a

21
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,640
ladder with us at the top.

22
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,440
And when we study it, we
err in both directions.

23
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:30,260
We see minds that aren't there,
and we miss minds that are.

24
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:32,820
Start with the two errors.

25
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:38,030
In the early nineteen hundreds, a horse in
Berlin called Clever Hans appeared to do

26
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:40,700
arithmetic, tapping out
answers with a hoof.

27
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:46,550
The psychologist Oskar Pfungst showed that
Hans was reading tiny, involuntary cues

28
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:50,560
in his questioners' posture, which
relaxed when he reached the right number.

29
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:53,440
Hans was clever, just not at arithmetic.

30
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:58,810
The corrective principle, called Morgan's
canon after the psychologist Conwy Lloyd

31
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:03,410
Morgan, says not to credit a behavior
to a higher faculty if a lower one would

32
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:09,490
explain it. Clever Hans is the founding
parable of machine learning evaluation, too.

33
00:02:09,540 --> 00:02:14,890
A model that scores well by exploiting
a spurious cue in its data is literally

34
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:16,960
called a Clever Hans predictor.

35
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:19,240
And he'll be back when we get to machines.

36
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:24,400
The opposite error is refusing to credit
a mind even when the evidence is strong.

37
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:30,030
The primatologist Frans de Waal called
it anthropodenial, and argued that the

38
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:35,650
history of animal cognition is largely a
history of humans testing animals badly and

39
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:37,060
then underestimating them.

40
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:42,070
Part of the problem is what the biologist
Jakob von Uexküll called the Umwelt, an

41
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:43,920
animal's own perceptual world.

42
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:48,800
A tick waiting on a branch responds to
little more than the smell of mammal skin,

43
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:51,020
warmth, and the texture of hair.

44
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:56,710
A test designed around human senses and
human interests can easily miss what another

45
00:02:56,760 --> 00:03:01,590
animal is good at. So every result in
this episode comes with the same question

46
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:04,980
attached: which of the two
errors is it most at risk of?

47
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:10,660
Begin with the birds. New Caledonian
crows don't just use tools;

48
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:15,930
in two thousand eighteen, a team showed
them assembling compound tools, fitting

49
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:20,190
short pieces together into one long enough
to reach a reward, without having been

50
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:22,500
shown how. Ravens appear to plan:

51
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:28,310
in two thousand seventeen, Can Kabadayi
and Mathias Osvath showed that ravens would

52
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:33,550
select a tool, or a token they could later
trade for food, and keep it for use after

53
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:38,740
a delay as long as a night, performing about
as well as great apes on similar tasks.

54
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:41,580
And scrub jays seem to model other minds.

55
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:47,150
In a classic study by Nathan Emery and
Nicola Clayton, jays that had been watched

56
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:51,810
while hiding food often came back alone
and moved it, but only if they had stolen

57
00:03:51,860 --> 00:03:56,480
from others themselves. Experienced
thieves seemed to anticipate theft.

58
00:03:56,920 --> 00:04:00,120
How do birds manage this with
brains the size of a walnut?

59
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:06,315
Part of the answer came in two thousand
sixteen, when Seweryn Olkowicz, Suzana

60
00:04:06,365 --> 00:04:10,790
Herculano-Houzel and colleagues counted
neurons in the brains of twenty eight bird

61
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:16,550
species. Parrots and songbirds pack neurons
about twice as densely as primates with

62
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:22,050
brains of the same mass. A large macaw's
forebrain holds at least as many neurons as

63
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,880
a macaque monkey's cortex, in
a brain a fraction of the size.

64
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:31,310
Corvids still fall well short of great
apes, so the claim is not that crows equal

65
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:35,980
chimpanzees. It's that a small brain can
carry a great deal of computing power.

66
00:04:36,380 --> 00:04:40,570
Birds also lack the layered cortex that
mammals have, which long made their

67
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:42,000
intelligence look like a puzzle.

68
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:47,530
In two thousand twenty, Martin Stacho,
Onur Güntürkün and colleagues found that

69
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:52,030
parts of the bird forebrain contain
repeating, column-like circuits strikingly

70
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:55,840
similar to the organization of our
cortex, though not in every region.

71
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:01,230
Then, in February two thousand twenty
five, a set of papers in Science traced how

72
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:02,640
these bird circuits develop.

73
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:07,410
The circuits arise from different developmental
origins, and mostly different gene

74
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:12,180
programs, than the mammalian cortex,
though a few neuron types do look shared.

75
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:17,150
Birds and mammals last shared an ancestor
around three hundred and twenty million

76
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:20,680
years ago. What they share
is not an inherited cortex.

77
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:24,440
It's largely a convergent
solution, arrived at twice.

78
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:28,860
Biologists treat convergence as
nature's own replication study.

79
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:32,380
Eyes evolved many times,
and so did flight.

80
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:37,990
When unrelated lineages arrive at similar
answers, it suggests the answer is shaped

81
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:40,160
by the problem more than by the ancestry.

82
00:05:40,520 --> 00:05:44,800
That is a claim about organization,
not about any particular tissue.

83
00:05:45,780 --> 00:05:48,160
The octopus pushes the point further.

84
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:54,330
Octopuses are mollusks, closer to clams
and snails than to us, and the last common

85
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:58,740
ancestor of octopuses and humans lived
perhaps six hundred million years ago,

86
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:02,180
probably a small,
simple, worm-like animal.

87
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:07,380
Everything sophisticated about octopus
cognition evolved separately from ours.

88
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:10,020
Separately is doing a lot of work there.

89
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:16,100
Octopuses still have neurons, synapses and
many of the same molecular parts we do.

90
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:18,480
True, and it's worth being precise.

91
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:22,760
The building blocks are shared,
inherited from that distant ancestor:

92
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:27,300
nerve cells, the basic signaling
machinery, many of the same genes.

93
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:31,890
When the octopus genome was sequenced in
two thousand fifteen, it even showed a

94
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:36,570
large expansion of a gene family called
the protocadherins, which help wire up

95
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:41,120
neurons, a family that had also
expanded, independently, in vertebrates.

96
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:44,600
What evolved separately is
the large-scale architecture:

97
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:48,180
how many neurons, how they're
organized, and what they do.

98
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:50,360
Same bricks, different buildings.

99
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:55,710
An octopus has about five hundred million
neurons, and about two-thirds of them are

100
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:57,840
in its arms rather than its central brain.

101
00:06:58,120 --> 00:07:02,040
The arms carry out complex
actions semi-independently.

102
00:07:02,090 --> 00:07:08,330
Octopuses solve puzzles, escape from tanks,
recognize individual people, and appear

103
00:07:08,380 --> 00:07:13,690
to play. Their relatives, cuttlefish, passed
a version of the marshmallow test in a

104
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:16,720
two thousand twenty one study
led by Alexandra Schnell.

105
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:21,460
They would pass up a less preferred food
to get a preferred one later, waiting

106
00:07:21,510 --> 00:07:25,710
anywhere from under a minute to more than
two minutes, and the best waiters were

107
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:27,300
also the fastest learners.

108
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:29,880
There's a puzzle in
their life history, too.

109
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:35,710
Most octopuses live only a year or two,
and many die soon after breeding, so the

110
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:37,300
young never meet their parents.

111
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:42,240
In our own lineage, big brains go
with long lives and long childhoods.

112
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:47,090
A two thousand nineteen review by Piero
Amodio, Nicola Clayton and colleagues

113
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:51,000
suggested that losing the protective
shell drove cephalopod intelligence:

114
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:56,780
once exposed, these animals needed flexible
ways to hunt and to avoid being eaten.

115
00:07:57,300 --> 00:08:01,830
Whatever the right answer turns out to be,
it's a different route to a large brain,

116
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:06,470
under different pressures, which is exactly
what you'd expect if intelligence is a

117
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:08,820
family of solutions rather than a ladder.

118
00:08:09,180 --> 00:08:13,640
The philosopher Peter Godfrey-Smith, who
has spent years diving with octopuses,

119
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:18,250
wrote that meeting one is probably
the closest we will come to meeting an

120
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:23,190
intelligent alien. We'll keep returning to
the octopus, in the philosophy of episode

121
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:28,210
nine, the evidence of episode ten and the
machine debates of episodes twelve and

122
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:32,630
thirteen, because it shows that complex
cognition can be built on a radically

123
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:37,380
different plan. Insects push
the size question to an extreme.

124
00:08:37,820 --> 00:08:42,770
A honeybee brain has about a million neurons,
nearly a hundred thousand times fewer

125
00:08:42,820 --> 00:08:48,150
than ours. Yet honeybees perform the
waggle dance, the symbolic communication of

126
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:52,177
direction and distance that won Karl von
Frisch a share of the nineteen seventy

127
00:08:52,227 --> 00:08:56,180
three Nobel Prize. How can
so few neurons do so much?

128
00:08:56,340 --> 00:09:01,630
In a two thousand nine review, Lars
Chittka and Jeremy Niven argued that many

129
00:09:01,680 --> 00:09:06,990
abilities we associate with big brains
need surprisingly small circuits, and that

130
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:12,040
bigger brains often buy more detail,
more memory and more parallel processing,

131
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:14,700
rather than qualitatively
new kinds of thinking.

132
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:17,410
Bumblebees show something
closer to culture.

133
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:23,130
In two thousand sixteen, Sylvain Alem, Lars
Chittka and colleagues hid an artificial

134
00:09:23,180 --> 00:09:27,500
flower under a clear plastic sheet,
reachable only by pulling a string.

135
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:32,280
Left to themselves, just two of two
hundred and ninety one bees solved it.

136
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:36,870
But most bees that watched a trained
demonstrator learned it, and the skill spread

137
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:39,900
through colonies. The
mechanism matters, though.

138
00:09:40,420 --> 00:09:44,510
Observers seem to have been drawn to the
string, and then to have learned by trial

139
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:49,770
and error. That is not imitation of the
pulling movement, and Morgan's canon says to

140
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:51,260
take that reading seriously.

141
00:09:51,580 --> 00:09:54,840
In two thousand twenty four,
Chittka's group went further.

142
00:09:55,040 --> 00:10:00,450
They built a two-step puzzle box that no
bee solved on its own, over twelve days or

143
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:04,340
more of access, yet several bees
learned it from trained demonstrators.

144
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:10,010
That's social learning of a behavior too
complex to invent alone, which in humans we

145
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:12,300
treat as the seed of cumulative culture.

146
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:17,950
And in two thousand twenty two, the same
lab reported that bumblebees rolled small

147
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:22,810
balls over and over with no reward, and
came to prefer a colored chamber they

148
00:10:22,860 --> 00:10:24,220
associated with the balls.

149
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:28,900
Play, in an insect. That's exactly
where I'd reach for Morgan's canon.

150
00:10:29,220 --> 00:10:31,320
So did the authors, by design.

151
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:35,720
They checked the standard criteria
animal behaviorists use for play:

152
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:41,230
the behavior is voluntary, not directly
rewarded, repeated, and done when the animal

153
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:43,080
is relaxed rather than stressed.

154
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:46,600
Whether anything is enjoyed
is a question for part two.

155
00:10:46,940 --> 00:10:49,260
As behavior, it met the criteria.

156
00:10:50,340 --> 00:10:54,000
Is there a general intelligence
in animals, like human g?

157
00:10:54,540 --> 00:10:56,840
Within species, increasingly yes.

158
00:10:57,640 --> 00:11:01,790
Louis Matzel and colleagues found that mice
good at one kind of learning tend to be

159
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:06,010
good at others, with a general factor
accounting for roughly two-fifths of the

160
00:11:06,060 --> 00:11:07,680
differences on their test batteries.

161
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:13,470
Across species, Simon Reader, Yfke Hager
and Kevin Laland compiled reports of

162
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:18,950
innovation, social learning and tool use
for sixty two primate species, and found

163
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:23,690
that a single dimension captured most of
the variation, with high scores evolving

164
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:26,080
independently in several lineages:

165
00:11:26,130 --> 00:11:29,410
capuchins, baboons,
macaques and great apes.

166
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:33,820
In a two thousand seventeen paper in the
journal Behavioral and Brain Sciences,

167
00:11:34,520 --> 00:11:39,640
Judith Burkart, Michael Schubiger and Carel
van Schaik drew these strands together,

168
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:44,950
arguing that general intelligence is not
unique to humans and linking its evolution

169
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,340
to how much animals
learn from one another.

170
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:51,770
But the measurement problems are serious,
and they're the problems of episode one in

171
00:11:51,820 --> 00:11:56,360
new clothes. In animal studies, being
keen on food can look like being clever.

172
00:11:57,140 --> 00:12:00,800
Samples are often small and drawn
from a single laboratory strain.

173
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,100
And species differ in
what they're good at.

174
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:08,470
In two thousand seven, Esther Herrmann and
colleagues gave the same broad battery to

175
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:12,240
two-and-a-half-year-old children,
chimpanzees and orangutans.

176
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:17,570
On physical problems, about tools, quantities
and space, the toddlers and the apes

177
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:22,730
performed similarly. On social problems,
such as following someone's gaze or

178
00:12:22,780 --> 00:12:25,820
learning by watching, the
toddlers were far ahead.

179
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:28,780
Humans don't sit at the
top of a single ladder.

180
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:32,000
We are unusually specialized
for learning from each other.

181
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:34,280
Brains tell the same mixed story.

182
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:39,090
In two thousand fourteen, Evan MacLean
and dozens of collaborators tested

183
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:43,220
self-control in five hundred and sixty
seven animals from thirty six species.

184
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:48,250
Absolute brain size predicted performance
better than brain size relative to the

185
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:53,310
body. And among primates, the breadth of
a species' diet predicted self-control,

186
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:55,660
while the size of its
social groups did not.

187
00:12:56,360 --> 00:12:58,400
Different pressures
produce different profiles.

188
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:01,700
Now go below the level of brains.

189
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:05,920
The biologist Michael Levin studies
what he calls basal cognition:

190
00:13:06,340 --> 00:13:08,640
problem solving by cells and tissues.

191
00:13:08,940 --> 00:13:14,530
His framework, called TAME, borrows an
old criterion from William James, that

192
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:18,340
intelligence is the ability to reach
the same goal by different means.

193
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:23,850
Levin's lab studies bioelectricity, the
patterns of voltage across cell membranes

194
00:13:23,900 --> 00:13:26,120
that help coordinate how tissues grow.

195
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:30,380
Flatworms called planaria can
regrow a whole body from a fragment.

196
00:13:30,760 --> 00:13:35,610
In one experiment, briefly blocking the
electrical connections between cells made

197
00:13:35,660 --> 00:13:40,390
some fragments regrow with two heads, and
those worms kept regrowing two heads when

198
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:42,420
cut again, with no further treatment.

199
00:13:42,760 --> 00:13:46,660
Even some treated worms that looked normal
grew two heads when they were cut later,

200
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:48,980
as if a stored pattern had been rewritten.

201
00:13:49,820 --> 00:13:54,490
Levin's group has also built xenobots
from frog cells and anthrobots from human

202
00:13:54,540 --> 00:13:58,020
airway cells, small living
constructs that move on their own.

203
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:02,610
What they show about cognition, as
opposed to the remarkable plasticity of

204
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:04,780
development, is still argued over.

205
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,860
Even a single cell can
solve spatial problems.

206
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:14,010
The slime mold Physarum polycephalum, one
giant cell with many nuclei, finds short

207
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:19,570
paths through mazes. In a two thousand ten
experiment, researchers placed oat flakes

208
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:24,210
in the positions of the cities around
Tokyo, and the slime mold grew a network of

209
00:14:24,260 --> 00:14:28,960
tubes that closely matched the efficiency
and resilience of the Tokyo rail system.

210
00:14:29,220 --> 00:14:31,380
That's a distributed optimizer.

211
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:33,600
I'm not sure I'd call it intelligent.

212
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:37,360
That's the live debate, and
it's partly about vocabulary.

213
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:40,390
Levin would say intelligence
comes in degrees:

214
00:14:40,440 --> 00:14:46,300
how well a system navigates toward goals
in some space, whether physical, anatomical

215
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:51,580
or behavioral. Critics reply that stretching
the word to slime molds empties it.

216
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:53,840
Both sides agree about the phenomena.

217
00:14:55,040 --> 00:14:59,690
Plants are where that disagreement gets
heated, and where a failed replication is

218
00:14:59,740 --> 00:15:05,510
instructive. A century ago, the Indian
physicist Jagadish Chandra Bose built

219
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:10,410
sensitive instruments to record electrical
responses in plants, and argued that they

220
00:15:10,460 --> 00:15:15,350
had something like nervous reactions, ideas
that were partly ahead of their time and

221
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:19,980
partly overstated. Today we know that
plants signal electrically and chemically.

222
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:25,770
A Venus flytrap effectively counts touches,
snapping shut after two and starting

223
00:15:25,820 --> 00:15:31,230
digestion after more. The sensitive plant
Mimosa stops folding its leaves when it is

224
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:34,620
repeatedly dropped without harm,
which looks like habituation.

225
00:15:34,980 --> 00:15:40,070
In two thousand sixteen, Monica Gagliano
and colleagues reported that pea seedlings

226
00:15:40,120 --> 00:15:43,450
could learn to associate a breeze
with light, a form of conditioning.

227
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:49,870
In two thousand twenty, a replication by
the plant biologist Kasey Markel found no

228
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:55,190
evidence of it. The original team argued
that his setup differed in key ways, and he

229
00:15:55,240 --> 00:15:58,260
replied that those differences
could not explain the result.

230
00:15:58,420 --> 00:16:03,050
And in two thousand nineteen, Lincoln Taiz
and colleagues argued that plants neither

231
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:05,140
possess nor require consciousness.

232
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:10,360
Most plant scientists accept sophisticated
signaling and adaptive behavior.

233
00:16:10,860 --> 00:16:13,630
Far fewer accept talk of plant minds.

234
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:18,230
Here the error at risk was over-attribution,
and so far the claim has not held up

235
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:19,560
under independent testing.

236
00:16:20,820 --> 00:16:22,840
Finally, minds made of many.

237
00:16:23,040 --> 00:16:27,930
A honeybee swarm chooses a new home through
a process the biologist Thomas Seeley

238
00:16:27,980 --> 00:16:29,920
described in Honeybee Democracy.

239
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:36,250
Scouts advertise candidate sites by dancing,
support builds, and the swarm commits

240
00:16:36,300 --> 00:16:41,290
when a quorum forms, a process that
resembles the way neurons accumulate evidence

241
00:16:41,340 --> 00:16:44,740
toward a decision. Human groups
may have something similar.

242
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:49,470
Anita Woolley and colleagues have proposed
a collective intelligence factor for

243
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:53,830
groups, and a two thousand twenty one
meta-analysis found that how a group

244
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:58,120
collaborates predicts that factor about
twice as well as the skills of its members,

245
00:16:58,540 --> 00:17:02,660
though whether groups really have a
general factor at all is still argued over.

246
00:17:03,900 --> 00:17:07,580
Crows, octopuses, bees,
slime molds, swarms.

247
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:09,720
What ties the menagerie together?

248
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:13,800
Organization. Intelligence
is multiply realized.

249
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:19,790
It evolved separately in birds, cephalopods
and insects, on different body plans and

250
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:24,200
brain architectures, and evolution
keeps converging on similar solutions.

251
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:29,370
That's one of the strongest arguments that
what matters is organization rather than

252
00:17:29,420 --> 00:17:33,760
any one kind of tissue, and we'll test
that argument hard in parts two and three.

253
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:35,840
It's a family, not a ladder.

254
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:40,710
Species differ in profile, and the human
specialty seems to be learning from one

255
00:17:40,760 --> 00:17:43,260
another. And our methods err both ways.

256
00:17:43,620 --> 00:17:48,330
Clever Hans, the doubts about Betty, and
the pea seedlings warn against seeing too

257
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:53,320
much. Anthropodenial, the neuron counts and
the bees warn against seeing too little.

258
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:56,600
The same tension will haunt
every claim about machines.

259
00:17:56,980 --> 00:18:01,390
So if intelligence takes this many
forms, can it be defined at all, without

260
00:18:01,440 --> 00:18:04,510
reference to any particular
species, body or test?

261
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:07,960
That's the next episode:
intelligence, formally.
